  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What are carotenoids?",
      "a": "Carotenoids are yellow, orange and red pigments made by plants, algae and some bacteria. They colour carrots, tomatoes and autumn leaves. Animals cannot make them, so they must eat them.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are carrots orange?",
        "What makes flamingos pink?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a flamingo eat?",
      "a": "It puts its head upside down in the water and pumps water in and out with its thick tongue. Rows of comb-like plates along its beak trap tiny algae, shrimp and insect larvae while the water drains out.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do flamingos eat upside down?",
        "What do flamingos eat?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does food turn a flamingo pink?",
      "a": "Enzymes in the liver change the carotenoids from the flamingo's food into pink and red pigments. These travel in the blood and are laid down in new feathers as they grow.",
      "asks": [
        "Would a flamingo turn white without its food?",
        "Do flamingos lose their colour?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Are baby flamingos pink?",
      "a": "No. Chicks hatch with grey or white fluffy down and take about two to three years to become fully pink, as they eat the right food. Parents feed them a red crop milk at first.",
      "asks": [
        "When do flamingos turn pink?",
        "What is flamingo crop milk?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does being pink matter to a flamingo?",
      "a": "A bright pink flamingo shows it is healthy and well fed, so brighter birds attract mates more easily. Some flamingos even rub pigment from a gland near the tail onto their feathers, like make-up.",
      "asks": [
        "Do flamingos wear make-up?",
        "Why are some flamingos brighter than others?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Flamingos get their pink colour from carotenoid pigments in the algae and brine shrimp they eat.",
    "Flamingo chicks hatch grey or white and take about two to three years to turn pink."
